## 9. IMPLEMENTED (2026-09-14) — what actually landed

Owner instruction: *"A is the right option"*, then *"ok just fix this"*. Landed
during a window in which **nothing heavy was running** — the P32 gate had
already taken SIGTERM at 19:14:05 (75,226 units flushed, resumable) and the
C29 coverage pass had finished. Verified before touching anything.

### 9.1 The mint — four sites, not two

The plan said two mint sites. There are **four**: `dict` as well as `list`, in
both runtimes. All four now route through **one** function per runtime:

| file | change |
|---|---|
| `src/ruby_runtime/base.rb` | **new** `SymbolicVar.len_var_name(name)` + `LEN_PREFIX = "SYM_LEN_"`, with the full rationale |
| `src/ruby_runtime/list.rb` | mints via `SymbolicVar.len_var_name`; doc comments updated |
| `src/ruby_runtime/dict.rb` | same |
| `src/py_runtime/base.py` | **new** `len_var_name()` + `LEN_PREFIX`, mirroring Ruby exactly |
| `src/py_runtime/list_.py` | mints via `len_var_name`; doc comment updated |
| `src/py_runtime/dict_.py` | same |

`grep 'len(#{'` over `ruby_runtime/` and `f"len({'` over `py_runtime/` now
return nothing. Both runtimes mint `SYM_LEN_X` — **exactly what
`fix_len_names` already produced**, so every existing `coverage_summary.json`,
demand file and probe bank stays valid (§8.2).

### 9.2 The seed shim — the half that makes it safe

`seed_for` matches exactly and falls back to `default` **silently**, so
without this every pre-rename seed would go quietly inert. Added to **all 8**
`concolic_targets.rb` copies, byte-identical (Rule T):

* `SEED_MISS` sentinel — keeps "seeded with `nil`/`false`" distinct from "not
  seeded", which `fetch(k, default)` got right and a naive rewrite loses.
* `length_var?`, `seed_aliases`, `seed_lookup` — both spellings accepted,
  **asked-for spelling always wins**, sibling consulted only on a miss.
* `seed_for` rewritten in each of its **three** pre-existing variants,
  preserving each: standard, `seeds_used` bookkeeping (conversations_index),
  and suffix-seeding (notifications_index).
* The 11 `seed_for("len(#{vn})", 1)` call sites now ask
  `seed_for(SymbolicVar.len_var_name(vn), 1)` — tied to the single source of
  truth rather than re-spelling the name by hand.

**One behaviour change was found and deliberately suppressed.** Under the old
spelling a length name ended with `)`, so a suffix seed could never match one.
`SYM_LEN_X_rows` ends with a bare suffix and **would** start matching,
silently widening what a suffix seed captures. The suffix loop now skips
length vars, preserving pre-rename behaviour exactly. Suffix-seeding a
cardinality dimension is a separate decision, not a side effect of a rename.

### 9.3 The single ingest point + the lint

`src/concolic_engine/dump_ingest.py` — `fix_len_names`, `canonicalize_ordinals`,
`normalize`, `load`, `check_invariants`. Guarantees, **asserted not documented**:

1. `concolic_seeds` is **never** rewritten, and is preserved verbatim under
   `RAW_SEEDS_KEY` (same key/meaning as `assumption_checker`'s, so a dump
   normalised here satisfies `seeds_as_written`).
2. A dump whose seeds were rewritten, or that holds one variable under both
   spellings, raises `MixedSpelling` — the half-converted state of §3.1.
3. The rename is idempotent and total, so **old and new dumps normalise to the
   same text**. This is the mixed-corpus invariant: without it one program
   path would split into two signatures and the execution tree would silently
   double.

**Correction to §4/Option A:** the plan's proposed invariant *"no `SYM_LEN_`
key in `concolic_seeds`"* is **wrong now** — under the new runtime seeds are
legitimately `SYM_LEN_`-spelled. The implemented invariant is the one that
actually holds in both worlds: *the ingest must not rewrite seeds at all.*

### 9.4 Tests

| suite | result |
|---|---|
| `src/test_name_boundary_seed.rb` (**new**, 14 tests) | 14 pass |
| `src/test_dump_ingest.py` (**new**, 22 tests) | 22 pass |
| `src/test_ruby_runtime.rb` | 65 pass (was 60 pass / 5 fail) |
| `ruby_runtime/test_list_rep.rb` | pass |
| `src/test_symbolic.py`, `py_runtime/test_list_rep.py` | 36 pass |

The 5 `test_ruby_runtime.rb` failures were the old-dialect assertions, updated
to the new spelling — plus the **property** is now pinned (`assert_match
/\A[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*\z/`), so a future rename cannot regress it silently.

The acceptance test of §8.6(3.i) is
`TestLegacySeedsStillTake#test_old_spelling_seed_is_honoured_by_a_new_spelling_lookup`
— and it asserts the **value that came back**, never merely the absence of an
error, because `.fetch(k, default)` makes "the seed works" and "the seed is
quietly defaulting" look identical from outside.

`TestNoHandRolledCopies` is the lint. It is **proven to fire** (it caught a
miscount of 62 vs a budget of 61 on first run, which is how the symlink
double-count below was found). Budget **60**, the measured baseline; it may
only go down as batches adopt `dump_ingest`.

### 9.5 Findings made during implementation

* **Four mint sites, not two** — `dict.rb` / `dict_.py` were missed by the
  plan's survey.
* **`reports/` contains symlinked directories.** The first lint counted 62
  copies where `grep` counted 60: one file reached by two paths. The lint now
  dedupes by real path. Any future census of `reports/` should do the same —
  the earlier §4.1 figures (48 definitions / 150 files) are file-list counts
  and are **one symlinked duplicate high**. True distinct: **47 files, 60
  definitions** (13 `canonicalize_ordinals` + 47 `fix_len_names`).
* **`concolic_engine/__init__.py` imports `run`/`solver`/`coverage`/
  `assumptions` eagerly**, so importing *any* submodule drags Z3 in. This
  defeats `names.py`'s docstring claim that it is "safe to import from
  anywhere, including from scripts that must not drag Z3 in" — that claim is
  **false today**. `dump_ingest`'s lazy import of `assumptions` is discipline,
  not yet a saving. Slimming that `__init__` is a separate, easy win.
* **Three pre-existing `seed_for` variants** across the eight supposedly
  verbatim-shared boundary copies (standard, `seeds_used`, suffix-seeding),
  each with a recorded reason. Preserved, not flattened — but worth knowing
  that the "verbatim shared boundary" is already not verbatim.
  `TestTargetBoundaryIsVerbatimShared` now pins the *shim* as identical across
  all eight, which is the part that must not drift.

### 9.6 NOT done — deliberately, and what it leaves open

* **`_canon_key()` does not hash the runtime or the target boundary** (§8.1).
  It remains possible to change how dumps are minted and have every probe bank
  silently reused. **This is the one hole this change leaves open**, and it is
  now slightly wider, because the runtime is a thing that can change. Closing
  it means editing `assumption_checker.py`, which is frozen at md5
  `5ae1b13c` and costs a full probe rebuild — so it belongs with the next
  batched edit (staged §E/§G), not here.
* **No batch converted to `dump_ingest` yet.** The 60 copies still run; the
  module and the lint's descending budget are what retire them, at each
  batch's own restart (§5 Phase 2).
* **No corpus regenerated, no summary rewritten, no probe bank discarded.**
  None is required (§8.2).
* **`assumption_checker.py` untouched** — verified md5 `5ae1b13c` after the
  change.

### 9.7 A gap found AFTER the runtime fix looked complete

Fixing the runtimes is **not sufficient**, and this is the most transferable
finding of the whole exercise.

A batch's wall mocks can mint a length variable **themselves**, bypassing
`len_var_name` entirely. Two such sites existed in
`results3/notifications_index/targets.rb`:

```ruby
aln   = "len(#{abase}_rows)"          # l.749 — hand-built, then symint(aln, ...)
len_v = symint("len(#{vn})", len_n, note: sql)   # l.1010
```

A hand-built name is a legal Z3 symbol and an **illegal Python identifier**, so
it silently reintroduces the dialect — and `solver.py` reads the resulting
declaration as **UNSAT**, i.e. as *"infeasible"*, the direction that
manufactures a false completeness claim. Both now mint through
`SymbolicVar.len_var_name`; verified resolvable from the nested module context
they sit in, and the file's syntax re-checked.

`test_dump_ingest.TestNoHandRolledCopies.test_no_live_batch_mints_a_hand_built_length_name`
now forbids the class across every live `.rb` under `reports/`, excluding the
frozen `results2/` and `results/` trees and `_bak_*` snapshots (records, never
executed). **It is proven to fire** — it found these two, then four more
offenders that turned out to be backups, which is how the exclusion list was
derived rather than guessed.

The 11 remaining `ct.seed_for("len(#{vn})", 1)` **lookups** in results3
`targets.rb` are deliberately left asking in the old spelling: existing seed
files are `len(`-keyed, so that is a direct hit rather than an aliased one, and
the shim covers the other direction. They are lookups, not mints — no dump
content depends on them.

### 9.8 Final state

| check | result |
|---|---|
| `assumption_checker.py` md5 | **`5ae1b13c`** — unchanged |
| campaigns disturbed | none — both had already finished/exited before any edit |
| corpora regenerated | none |
| probe banks discarded | none |
| runtimes minting a Python-illegal name | none (`grep` clean in both) |
| live batches minting one by hand | none (lint enforces) |
| new tests | 37 (14 Ruby + 23 Python), all green |
| end-to-end proof | old mint `sat=False`; new mint `sat=True`; old dump via `dump_ingest` `sat=True` with seeds preserved |

### 9.9 The DSE runners — found by asking "is this only a src/ change?"

**It was not.** `run_dse.rb` decides how to flip a branch by matching the path
condition's **expression text**, and five of the six runners matched only the
OLD spelling:

```ruby
if (m = /\A\(len\((.+)\) != 0\)\z/m.match(s))        # posts_show:286
   /\A\(len\((.+)\) (<=|>=|==|!=|<|>) (-?\d+)\)\z/m   # people_show:496
   /\A\((len\(.+\)) > 1\)\z/m                        # comments_index:467
```

(`notifications_index:332` already accepted both — someone hit this before.)

All five now normalise on the way in via a `canon_len` helper, so the matchers
stay written in one spelling and seed keys stay `len(...)`-spelled (which
`seed_for` accepts either way). **Differential, every runner × every length
operator, old vs new spelling: 11/11 MATCH.**

**Measured severity, and it is LESS than I first said.** I called this
"campaign-breaking" before measuring. Replaying the pre-fix runners against the
new spelling:

| outcome | endpoints | actually broken? |
|---|---|---|
| generic `(VAR op LIT)` matcher caught it, emitting a `SYM_LEN_X`-keyed seed | all five, most operators | **No** — `seed_for` asks `SYM_LEN_X` and the shim hits it directly. Semantics (incl. `want_taken`) verified identical. |
| no matcher at all -> `nil`, decision **unflippable** | `comments_index` `(len(X) > 1)` | **Yes** — the "two or more" cardinality decision would silently stop being explored |
| old was already `nil`, new produces a seed | `posts_show` `== 0`, `people_stream` `> 1` | No — an improvement, not a regression |

So the genuine breakage was **one endpoint, one operator**. The rest was
rescued by the `seed_for` shim, which turns out to have been load-bearing in a
way §9.2 did not anticipate: without it, every "wrong key" row above would
have failed silently. The runner fix is still correct and still worth having —
it makes the runners spelling-independent rather than accidentally saved.

**Two harness errors made during this check, recorded because both pointed the
reassuring way at first:**

* A mutation test reported "TEST HAS NO TEETH". False — it broke the
  `len(->SYM_LEN_` branch while the test exercises `SYM_LEN_->len(`. Per-branch
  retest: both caught.
* A shell `sed` with a greedy `.*` rewrote `(len(ROWS) != 0)` into the
  malformed `(SYM_LEN_ROWS) != 0`, which made the FIXED runners look broken.
  Redone in Ruby.

Neither changed a product conclusion, but both produced a confident wrong
number first. Substitutions over expression text are exactly what this whole
document is about; doing them by hand in a shell is the same mistake one level
out.

### 9.10 Experiment-side work still outstanding

`src/` is done. The experiment is not:

| item | state |
|---|---|
| 8 × `concolic_targets.rb` seed shim | **done** |
| `notifications_index/targets.rb` hand-built mints (2) | **done** |
| 5 × `run_dse.rb` flip matchers | **done** |
| 60 hand-rolled `fix_len_names`/`canonicalize_ordinals` -> `dump_ingest` | **not started** — the live defect (posts_show rewrites seed keys, notifications does not) is still in place |
| `coverage_report.py` / `gate_canon.py` per batch | not started — one probe bank each, at each batch's restart |
| `_canon_key()` extended to hash runtime + boundary | not done — needs the frozen checker |
| a real DSE round through JRuby confirming a live dump is `SYM_LEN_`-named and a live old seed file takes | **not run** |

The last row is the one I would do before trusting any of this on a campaign.
Everything above it is verified only by unit tests and extracted-source
differentials.
